-- title:
--   Components through a supersingular point have valuation local rings
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $M'$ a non-zero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. The field $L$ has characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is required by `hH₁` to be [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) intersected with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$; `levelH q M'` is itself the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, so $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L \subset \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133).
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with $A \to L$ making $L$ its fraction field, with $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$, non-zero, has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) (`hj`), and $\varpi \in A$ generates $\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hϖ`). The element $\varpi t$ (Lean `ϖt`) satisfies $\varpi t^{\,q^2-1} = q u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`). Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin` for $j$ viewed in $C$.
--
--   The ideal $y \subset C$ is maximal (`hy`), contains $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and is supersingular (`hss`): for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(\mathtt{jChartFin})$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $j \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no non-trivial $q$-torsion point.
--
--   A *level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$* means an $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for every weight $k$, every pair $f,g$ of modular forms of weight $k$ for [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$, $p_g$ having non-zero image, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficient-embedded ratio $p_f/p_g$, and every $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, the series $\iota$-pushed from $\tau x$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k \mathtt{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k \mathtt{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` (a rigidity-and-charts package, whose clauses are listed below) asserts: for every point $z$ of [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), every $\varpi_z$ in the stalk at $z$ equal to the germ of the pullback of $\varpi$ from `Spec A` and lying in the maximal ideal of that stalk, and every point $y'$ of `XFin A K j` $=\operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` and satisfying the same supersingularity condition as `hss` for $y'.\mathtt{asIdeal}$, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma \varpi)$, power series $f,u,v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f \equiv \mathtt{drinfeldForm}\,q\,W = X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma(\varpi t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f u)$, such that, writing $\mathrm{toC}$ for the map from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mkS}$ for the quotient map and $\mathrm{germY}$ for the induced map $C \to$ stalk at $z$ through $y'$: (i) $e$ carries the germ of $a \in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) every level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ maps $C$ into $C$; (iii) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, the induced endomorphism of $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathtt{asIdeal}$; (iv) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'$, there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, a scalar $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ with: $\theta$ intertwines $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ with $\tau$; $\theta$ fixes the classes of constants; $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$; $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$; $c \equiv 1$ when $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$; and $c \not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$; (v) two primes $P_1, P_2$ of $S$, each omitting one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and each containing a tangent form $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, have distinct contractions to the stalk along $e \circ \mathrm{toC}$ whenever $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$; (vi) for a prime $P$ as in (v) with tangent form $1 \cdot X_0 + 0 \cdot X_1 + h$, an element $a \in C$ has $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a))$ in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$; (vii) [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with this element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form takes unit values at all $(a,b) \in W^2$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, such that $e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\mathtt{jqNModC} - a_0)))$ is the class of $h$.
--
--   Next, specific data are fixed: a point $z$ with $\varpi_z$ in the maximal ideal of its stalk (`hϖz`, `hz`), a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ over $z$ (`hy'`) satisfying the supersingularity condition `hss'`, with $y'.\mathtt{asIdeal} = y$ (`hy'y`); a complete discrete valuation domain $W_1$ with $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$, $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$, series $f_1, u_1, v_1$ with $u_1, v_1$ units and $f_1 \equiv \mathtt{drinfeldForm}\,q\,W_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ with $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts, for this datum at the given $z$ and $y'$, exactly the seven clauses (i)–(vii) just listed.
--
--   The ideal $J \subset C$ is required by `hJ` to be the infimum of the ideals obtained, as $\gamma$ runs over $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ over level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, by pulling back along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1 \varpi t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. The $A$-subalgebra $B \subset K$ is required by `hB` to be the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K : x\,\varpi t \in J\}$, restricted to scalars in $A$. A valuation subring `W` of $K$ is given together with `hBW`: $B \subseteq$ `W`.
--
--   The remaining hypotheses are four packages. `hR1`: $C \le B$, and every element of $K$ is a quotient $g/h$ with $g,h \in B$, $h \ne 0$. `hR2`: $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$, and $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. `hR3` (five clauses): an element of $L$ lies in `W` exactly when it comes from $A$; the maximal ideal of `W` is generated by $\varpi$; `W` is a discrete valuation ring; for $b \in C$, $b \in y$ if and only if $b$ lies in the maximal ideal of `W`; and $f \in K$ lies in `W` exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g,h \in B$ and $h$ outside the maximal ideal of `W`. `hEQ` (five clauses): first, for every $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose vanishing locus is the maximal ideal of `W`, which is compatible with $A \to \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, and which is equivariant in the sense that for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and any level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving `W` there is $c \in \mathrm{GF}(q^2)^\times$ with $(\mathtt{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathtt{hAction}\,q\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,(\mathtt{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c) \circ \rho$ on $B$, with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$; second, level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B$; third, every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached from the maximal ideal of `W` by some such $\tau$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, in the sense that all $b \in B$ lying in the maximal ideal of `W` have $\tau b \in Q$; fourth, an element $b \in B$ whose images under all such $\tau$ (with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$) lie in the maximal ideal of `W` is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; fifth, a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on $C$ preserves `W`.
--
--   Finally, $\mathfrak{p} \subset C$ is a prime ideal containing $\varpi$ (`hϖ𝔭`) with $\mathfrak{p} \le y$ (`h𝔭y`) and $\mathfrak{p} \ne y$ (`h𝔭ne`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is: for all $c_1, c_2 \in C$ there exist $d, s \in C$ with $s \notin y$ and either $s c_1 - d c_2 \in \mathfrak{p}$ or $s c_2 - d c_1 \in \mathfrak{p}$.
--
--   The conclusion is the elementwise form of the assertion that the localisation at $y$ of $C/\mathfrak{p}$ is a valuation ring of its fraction field: any two elements become comparable up to a denominator invertible at $y$, which is the regularity of the special-fibre component $\mathfrak{p}$ through the supersingular point $y$ (the Igusa branches through a supersingular point, in the sense of Katz–Mazur). It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.end_blowupChart_eq_of_on_same_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.end_blowupChart_eq_of_on_same_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd) to identify the ends of the blow-up chart lying on one and the same component.
